WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:08.080
The aviation sector is on the brink of a crisis.&nbsp;
Its future is in limbo as the world moves towards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:13.840
decarbonisation. Planes are currently&nbsp;
only responsible for 2-3% of the world’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:20.320
carbon dioxide emissions, but that’s&nbsp;
expected to rise to 25% by 2050. [1]

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:25.920
Most major polluters have clear technology&nbsp;
pathways to a cleaner future. The automotive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:31.120
industry has batteries and electric motors.&nbsp;
The shipping industry has a range of potential&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:36.960
alternative clean fuels to choose from. Our&nbsp;
electrical grids are rapidly investing in solar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:42.960
and wind, and future nuclear energy projects&nbsp;
are being researched intensively. There is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:49.360
still plenty of work to do, but the path ahead&nbsp;
for these sectors has been surveyed and marked.

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:55.600
However, the aviation industry has no&nbsp;
clear way forward for replacing kerosene,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.600 --> 00:01:01.440
and if the aviation sector can’t find answers&nbsp;
to this problem, it’s projected that with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:07.840
continued growth of passenger numbers and the&nbsp;
expected decarbonisation across other industries,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:15.280
that it could represent as much as 25% of&nbsp;
total world wide emissions by 2050. [1]

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:21.200
To understand this problem, and the potential&nbsp;
technologies we could see in the future,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:26.080
we first need to understand the&nbsp;
current state of aviation fuel.

00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:29.360
Today, nearly all jet engines use kerosene,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:35.600
but internal combustion turbine engines are not&nbsp;
actually that picky about the fuel they consume.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:40.000
Gas powered turbines power grids all over&nbsp;
the world [2] , and many of them are being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:45.920
converted to run on bioethanol [3].Early jet&nbsp;
engines were powered by mostly gasoline. If it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:51.680
burns hot and can be pumped into a combustion&nbsp;
chamber, chances are it can drive a turbine.

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:56.000
But, it’s not quite so simple for a jet&nbsp;
engine that flies and carries humans.

00:01:56.640 --> 00:02:01.120
There are two main types of jet&nbsp;
fuel used for commercial aviation.

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:08.080
Jet A and Jet A-1. Jet A is primarily&nbsp;
used in the United States and Jet A-1&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:13.120
is used in the rest of the world. [4] 
 
So is this just another case of the United&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:18.400
States insisting on being different because they&nbsp;
are too stubborn to admit the rest of the world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:21.840
may just have a better system? In this case, no.

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:25.680
The primary difference between&nbsp;
the two is their freezing point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:33.520
with Jet A-1 having a lower freezing&nbsp;
point of -47 degrees versus Jet A at -40.

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:38.960
For domestic flights within the US, Jet&nbsp;
A’s freezing point is just fine, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:44.640
for colder climates, or colder international&nbsp;
routes like those that fly over the arctic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:50.320
a lower freezing point is needed to&nbsp;
prevent the fuel from turning to wax. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:54.400
a lower freezing point is&nbsp;
desirable, but it comes at a price.

00:02:54.960 --> 00:03:00.480
The United States uses Jet A because&nbsp;
it is cheaper. To understand why,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:04.240
we need to understand how crude oil is refined.

00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:08.640
Crude oil is essentially just a&nbsp;
blend of many different hydrocarbons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:13.840
all with different carbon chain lengths.&nbsp;
[5] We have short chain gas molecules like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:20.880
methane and butane, with 1-4 carbon atoms in each&nbsp;
chain. Then we have longer gasoline molecules,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:27.360
with chain lengths between 5 and 10. While,&nbsp;
kerosene molecules range from around 10 to 16.

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:32.960
We can separate each fuel type from crude oil&nbsp;
thanks to these chain lengths impacting the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.960 --> 00:03:38.960
boiling point of each component, which allows us&nbsp;
to separate them with fractional distillation.

00:03:38.960 --> 00:03:43.840
We simply heat the crude oil up and&nbsp;
pump it into a distillation tower.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:48.640
The longer chain hydrocarbons liquify&nbsp;
lower in the distillation tower,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:53.360
thanks to their lower boiling point, and&nbsp;
when they do so, they are tapped off.

00:03:53.360 --> 00:03:58.320
The shorter chain molecules will remain&nbsp;
gaseous and continue rising through the tower,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.320 --> 00:04:05.920
but the tower gets gradually colder as it rises.&nbsp;
Soon Kerosene will turn to liquid and be removed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.920 --> 00:04:11.520
then gasoline, and finally the lightest methane&nbsp;
and butane gases rise right to the very top.

00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:18.000
So how does this explain Jet A-1’s lower freezing&nbsp;
point? Freezing points and boiling points are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:23.680
generally linked, so Jet A-1 can lower its&nbsp;
freezing point by excluding hydrocarbons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:29.520
with longer chains, and therefore excludes&nbsp;
lower boiling point molecules from the mix.

00:04:29.520 --> 00:04:33.760
Jet A, in comparison, is less&nbsp;
picky about the freezing point&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.760 --> 00:04:39.280
and can take a larger cut of this distillate.&nbsp;
Meaning, there is a broader percentage of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.280 --> 00:04:45.040
the crude oil that can be included in&nbsp;
Jet A, making it cheaper than Jet A-1.

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:49.280
So, it makes perfect sense for a&nbsp;
country like the United States,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:52.720
that doesn’t need to worry too&nbsp;
much about low temperatures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.720 --> 00:04:58.320
to manufacture a cheaper wider cut fuel&nbsp;
for their domestic airline industry.

00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:05.520
So, these are our first two properties we need&nbsp;
to consider when choosing a future aviation fuel:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:12.000
freezing point and cost. The freezing point issue&nbsp;
rules out longer chain molecules like diesel.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:16.240
Diesel powered vehicles in Canada&nbsp;
and Alaska actually have to cut their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:20.480
fuel with kerosene to prevent the fuel&nbsp;
from freezing in the winter months. [6]

00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:27.440
This is the same reason a different jet fuel, Jet&nbsp;
B, is used in parts of Canada and Alaska. It’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:33.200
also known as wide-cut fuel, which gets its name&nbsp;
because it takes a much larger cut of the crude&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.200 --> 00:05:40.960
oil distillate, with a mix of 30% kerosene and 70%&nbsp;
gasoline, giving it an even lower freezing point&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:48.480
of -60. So if this wide-cut fuel can be used&nbsp;
in engines, why isn’t it used in all engines?

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:52.960
Gasoline, thanks to it’s&nbsp;
shorter carbon chain lengths,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.960 --> 00:05:59.200
is too volatile for general use in aviation.&nbsp;
It’s flash point is much lower than kerosene.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.200 --> 00:06:05.280
Flash point is the lowest temperature vapors can&nbsp;
form from a liquid to create an ignitable mixture&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:12.160
in air. So low flash points make unintended&nbsp;
explosions and fires much more likely,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.160 --> 00:06:18.640
not something airports and planes are particularly&nbsp;
fond of. The lower temperature of vaporization can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.640 --> 00:06:24.640
also cause problems with vapor locks in plumbing.&nbsp;
Where gas bubbles can form and cause blockages.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.640 --> 00:06:30.080
This becomes an even larger issue for jet&nbsp;
engines, as boiling points lower as pressures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.080 --> 00:06:37.280
decrease at altitude. So gasoline is not a&nbsp;
desirable jet fuel for general applications.

00:06:37.280 --> 00:06:43.200
The US Navy and US Airforce even use two&nbsp;
different Kerosene grades for a similar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:50.240
reason. The U.S. Air Force uses JP-8 [1], which&nbsp;
is similar to Jet A-1, but with the addition of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.240 --> 00:06:56.240
corrosion inhibitors and anti-icing additives&nbsp;
that are not required for the Jet A-1 standard.

00:06:56.240 --> 00:07:01.680
While the US Navy uses JP-5. The primary&nbsp;
difference between the NAVY and Air Force&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.680 --> 00:07:08.400
fuels is that the navy fuel has a higher&nbsp;
flash point. 60 degrees versus 38 degrees.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.400 --> 00:07:14.160
This makes it much safer to handle during&nbsp;
refueling operations on aircraft carriers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.160 --> 00:07:20.880
and makes explosions much less likely in the event&nbsp;
of an attack. This was a constant worry during WW2&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.440 --> 00:07:27.040
with the predominantly gasoline powered&nbsp;
piston engines. Fuel fires were not a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.040 --> 00:07:32.880
rare occurrence during the war. [7] This is the&nbsp;
third property we need to consider: flash points.

00:07:33.440 --> 00:07:38.960
But we aren’t done yet. We haven’t even&nbsp;
mentioned the most obvious. Energy content.

00:07:39.520 --> 00:07:44.960
The primary function of aviation turbine fuel&nbsp;
is to power the aircraft. This is achieved by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.960 --> 00:07:48.880
igniting the fuel, which releases&nbsp;
heat, which raises the pressure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.880 --> 00:07:54.880
which causes air flow. To fulfill this role&nbsp;
most effectively we want high energy content.

00:07:54.880 --> 00:07:59.120
We can measure the energy content of&nbsp;
a fuel pretty easily. It’s simply the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.120 --> 00:08:04.000
heat released when a known quantity of the&nbsp;
fuel is burned under specific conditions.

00:08:04.560 --> 00:08:08.560
There are two “quantity” measurements&nbsp;
however. Energy per unit mass,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.560 --> 00:08:15.040
measured in megajoules per kilogram, and energy&nbsp;
per unit volume, measured in megajoules per liter.

00:08:15.040 --> 00:08:20.400
In general a dense fuel with a high&nbsp;
volumetric energy content is desired,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.400 --> 00:08:25.680
especially for military aircraft that always take&nbsp;
off with their fuel tanks filled to the brim,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.680 --> 00:08:32.160
so volumetric energy density is a more important&nbsp;
metric. Commercial aircraft only fill their tanks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.160 --> 00:08:36.960
with enough fuel to reach their destination,&nbsp;
with a little extra in case of emergency,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.960 --> 00:08:41.280
but volumetric energy density is&nbsp;
still generally a better measurement.

00:08:41.920 --> 00:08:46.880
Let’s add this to our shopping list, and&nbsp;
start looking at potential alternative fuels.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.600 --> 00:08:52.400
First, let’s look at the numbers for our 4&nbsp;
main identified properties with a typical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.400 --> 00:08:58.080
kerosene jet fuel. Cost, freezing point,&nbsp;
flash point and volumetric energy density.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.640 --> 00:09:02.240
These will be our measuring&nbsp;
sticks for our alternative fuels.

00:09:02.800 --> 00:09:07.280
The first stop on our proverbial&nbsp;
shopping trip is the biofuel aisle.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.280 --> 00:09:10.320
We have a tonne of options to choose from here.

00:09:10.320 --> 00:09:14.240
In terms of production volumes,&nbsp;
bioethanol and biodiesel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.240 --> 00:09:16.480
are currently the most available biofuels.

00:09:17.040 --> 00:09:23.040
Ethanol is a short chain alcohol. Similar to&nbsp;
the short chain hydrocarbons, it’s freezing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.040 --> 00:09:30.320
and flash point is quite low, minus 115 degrees&nbsp;
celsius and 13 degrees respectively. [8] The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.320 --> 00:09:36.320
low freezing point is useful, but the low flash&nbsp;
point is a problem. This makes ethanol volatile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.320 --> 00:09:42.000
which makes it undesirable as a jet fuel.&nbsp;
It’s volumetric energy density is about 61%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:47.680
of kerosene, meaning range would be reduced&nbsp;
if fuel tanks remained the same size. [9]

00:09:47.680 --> 00:09:53.040
Biodiesel suffers from the opposite problem to&nbsp;
bioethanol because it’s carbon chain lengths&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.040 --> 00:10:00.480
are much longer. As a result it’s flash point is&nbsp;
very high, between 98 and 150 degrees depending on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.480 --> 00:10:07.280
the feedstock used, and as expected comes with&nbsp;
a very high freezing point of about 1 degrees.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.280 --> 00:10:11.040
This fuel would turn to wax in&nbsp;
the fuel tanks. It’s unusable.

00:10:11.680 --> 00:10:18.240
However, we can further process these biofuels to&nbsp;
create fuels that are so similar to kerosene that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.240 --> 00:10:24.000
they can even be used in current generation&nbsp;
planes with very little modification. [10]

00:10:24.000 --> 00:10:31.440
Airbus began testing a fuel composed entirely&nbsp;
of biofuel this year in an A350 powered by Rolls&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.440 --> 00:10:37.200
Royce XWB engines. [11] Testing the plane's&nbsp;
performance and emissions using the fuel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.200 --> 00:10:42.560
which was manufactured be Neste. A company&nbsp;
that manufactures biofuels from palm oil&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.560 --> 00:10:47.440
and waste oils, like cooking oil. Results&nbsp;
of this test have not yet been published,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.440 --> 00:10:53.600
but NASA has already published data from their&nbsp;
own tests with a 50-50 fuel blend or traditional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.600 --> 00:11:00.800
jet fuel and a similar plant oil derived biofuel.&nbsp;
[12] Their tests showed, with only a 50-50 blend,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.800 --> 00:11:07.280
that particulate emissions in the contrail&nbsp;
were reduced by up to 70%. That’s important,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.280 --> 00:11:11.840
because those particulates have a much&nbsp;
larger impact on earth’s atmosphere&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.840 --> 00:11:17.040
than the carbon emissions. This is&nbsp;
positive news, but these biofuels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.040 --> 00:11:23.040
are a long way from being cost effective or&nbsp;
even environmentally friendly to manufacture.

00:11:23.040 --> 00:11:29.040
The main challenges facing biofuels are scaling&nbsp;
the feedstocks in an environmentally friendly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.040 --> 00:11:36.400
way and cost. Waste oil products as feedstocks are&nbsp;
fantastic and every country should be working on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.400 --> 00:11:41.760
ways to collect waste products to feed this&nbsp;
growing industry, but sourcing oil from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.760 --> 00:11:48.160
palm oil industry is obviously problematic, as&nbsp;
the palm oil industry is driving the destruction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.160 --> 00:11:54.480
of the Borneo rainforest. Sourcing enough&nbsp;
feedstocks to completely replace fossil fuels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.480 --> 00:12:01.440
in the aviation industry is going to be a massive&nbsp;
problem to solve, and right now we have no answer.

00:12:02.080 --> 00:12:06.560
Cost is also a huge issue. Norway announced a 0.5%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.120 --> 00:12:11.600
biofuel mandate for the&nbsp;
aviation sector in 2019. [13]

00:12:11.600 --> 00:12:18.960
This is a tiny fraction of the total fuel used,&nbsp;
but Scandavian Airlines has said that this 0.5%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.960 --> 00:12:26.880
mandate will add an additional 3.3 million dollars&nbsp;
in fuel costs a year. Making it 100%, assuming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.880 --> 00:12:34.560
prices wouldn’t rise with the extra demand, would&nbsp;
cost 660 million dollars extra a year. That would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.560 --> 00:12:41.440
Completely wipe out Scandinavian Airlines'&nbsp;
2019 profit of 84 million dollars. [14] So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.440 --> 00:12:47.840
these biofuels currently fail the cost metric,&nbsp;
despite being suitable alternatives to kerosene.&nbsp;

00:12:47.840 --> 00:12:52.160
Even if we ignore the questionable&nbsp;
environmental benefit of the feedstocks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.160 --> 00:12:57.120
the real issue here is the difficulty&nbsp;
in scaling up feedstocks to meet demand.&nbsp;

00:12:57.120 --> 00:13:00.080
So, are there any other alternatives?&nbsp;

00:13:00.080 --> 00:13:03.840
Hydrogen is also being explored&nbsp;
as a potential future fuel.

00:13:04.400 --> 00:13:09.200
Airbus has published several concept&nbsp;
aircraft that could utilize hydrogen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:09.200 --> 00:13:16.000
because, unlike biofuels, hydrogen cannot be&nbsp;
used in existing planes. This would require a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.000 --> 00:13:22.160
complete overhaul of airlines plane inventories&nbsp;
and would cost trillions over several years.&nbsp;

00:13:22.720 --> 00:13:28.320
Hydrogen’s main advantage is that’s feedstock&nbsp;
is just water, and we are surrounded by water.&nbsp;

00:13:28.880 --> 00:13:34.240
However, hydrogen currently needs very&nbsp;
pure fresh water to prevent corrosion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:34.240 --> 00:13:39.440
to the electrodes that split the water&nbsp;
apart during electrolysis. Researchers are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:39.440 --> 00:13:44.720
working on ways to extend the life of these&nbsp;
electrodes while preventing the salt ions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.720 --> 00:13:49.280
like chloride, that are found in seawater,&nbsp;
from breaking down the electrodes. [15]

00:13:49.280 --> 00:13:54.960
The alternative is simply pairing the system&nbsp;
with desalination process, but this would draw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:54.960 --> 00:13:59.040
even more electricity for what is&nbsp;
already a very expensive process.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:59.760 --> 00:14:04.320
Hydrogen, right now, does not&nbsp;
satisfy our cost requirement.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:04.320 --> 00:14:09.440
But let’s move forward with the expectation&nbsp;
that we will have massive amounts of excess&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:09.440 --> 00:14:15.360
renewable energy looking for a home in the&nbsp;
future and assume these costs will come down.&nbsp;

00:14:15.360 --> 00:14:19.440
Hydrogen has insanely good&nbsp;
gravimetric energy density,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:19.440 --> 00:14:29.040
at 120 MJ/kg. [16] Completely blowing kerosene&nbsp;
out of the water at around 44 MJ/kg. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:29.040 --> 00:14:36.240
hydrogen’s volumetric energy density, the quantity&nbsp;
we actually care about, is complete dog trash.&nbsp;

00:14:36.240 --> 00:14:41.760
The only way to get it to a reasonable number&nbsp;
is by pressurizing it or making it cold,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:41.760 --> 00:14:48.960
but even then it’s volumetric energy density&nbsp;
is terrible. At 700 bar, that’s 700 times&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:48.960 --> 00:14:53.600
atmospheric pressure, hydrogen still&nbsp;
only has a volumetric energy density&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:53.600 --> 00:15:02.640
of 5.6 MJ/L, compared jet fuels 38.3 MJ/L. [17]
Pressurizing a fuel tank to 700 bar comes with its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:02.640 --> 00:15:09.200
dangers, as repeated pressure cycles can lead to&nbsp;
rapid failure due to fatigue. This is made worse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:09.200 --> 00:15:15.600
by hydrogen’s habit of attacking and embrittling&nbsp;
materials, a phenomenon that is also accelerated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:15.600 --> 00:15:21.920
by higher pressures. [18] So, most designs for&nbsp;
hydrogen fuel tanks instead call for cryogenic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:21.920 --> 00:15:27.280
storage. Where the hydrogen is cooled to&nbsp;
achieve a higher volumetric energy density&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:27.280 --> 00:15:32.960
with much lower pressures. [16] This also&nbsp;
results in higher energy densities of 8 MJ/L,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:33.840 --> 00:15:38.720
but still much lower than the&nbsp;
38 MJ/L of traditional fuels.&nbsp;

00:15:39.440 --> 00:15:45.120
This low volumetric energy density, and need&nbsp;
to pressurize, makes hydrogen fuel tanks a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:45.120 --> 00:15:51.280
nightmare to integrate to an aircrafts airframe.
Planes these days place a large amount of fuel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:51.280 --> 00:15:57.680
inside the wings. [19] This is ideal for several&nbsp;
reasons. It takes up no useful space inside the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:57.680 --> 00:16:03.200
cabin of the plane. Aircraft wings need to be&nbsp;
hollow to increase the strength of the wings.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:03.200 --> 00:16:08.640
The weight of the fuel being located so close to&nbsp;
the center of lift means the plane does not need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:08.640 --> 00:16:14.720
to adjust it’s control surfaces during flight&nbsp;
to compensate for changes in center of gravity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:14.720 --> 00:16:21.200
as the fuel gets used up, which reduces drag.
Finally, when flying, the wings deflect upwards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:21.200 --> 00:16:26.560
due to the upwards lift force they create. This&nbsp;
creates stress in the supporting structures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:26.560 --> 00:16:32.960
of the plane. So, by putting the fuel in the&nbsp;
wings it actually helps the wings deflect less&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:32.960 --> 00:16:38.000
as the weight of the fuel pushes them&nbsp;
down, and as the fuel is used up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:38.000 --> 00:16:43.600
the lift the wings need to generate reduces, and&nbsp;
the upwards lift forcing the wings up reduces.&nbsp;

00:16:44.800 --> 00:16:50.480
Storing the heavy fuel in the wings is an&nbsp;
incredibly elegant solution, and it’s not possible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:50.480 --> 00:16:56.320
with hydrogen. There simply is not enough space&nbsp;
in the narrow hollow structure of wings to fit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:56.320 --> 00:17:02.640
the equipment needed. This space is also getting&nbsp;
even smaller as newer generation composite planes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:02.640 --> 00:17:07.920
enter the market [19], with their sleek elegant&nbsp;
wings being much thinner than older metal versions&nbsp;

00:17:08.480 --> 00:17:13.760
Because hydrogen needs to be pressurized and&nbsp;
cooled, it requires specialized fuel tanks that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:13.760 --> 00:17:20.000
are too bulky to fit into these small spaces. The&nbsp;
matter is only made worse because of hydrogen’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:20.000 --> 00:17:26.080
dismal volumetric energy density. Some designs for&nbsp;
hydrogen planes simply call for the massive fuel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:26.080 --> 00:17:31.680
tanks to be placed inside the fuselage, replacing&nbsp;
valuable space that could be used for passengers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:31.680 --> 00:17:38.000
or cargo. This just compounds the issue of cost&nbsp;
even more, as airlines will now be making less,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:38.000 --> 00:17:43.680
while also having to pay more for fuel.
While some have proposed a more drastic change in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:43.680 --> 00:17:50.160
flight architecture, the blended wing. The blended&nbsp;
wing offers fantastic drag characteristics and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:50.160 --> 00:17:55.920
leaves plenty of space within the wing to store&nbsp;
the large fuel tanks. There is a lot more to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:55.920 --> 00:18:01.360
said about this design, but we will explore this&nbsp;
kind of plane in more detail in a future video.&nbsp;

00:18:01.920 --> 00:18:08.000
Now we need to deal with the safety concerns.&nbsp;
Hydrogen is a gas in normal conditions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:08.000 --> 00:18:14.320
so flash point is not a relevant quantity.&nbsp;
It’s gases are going to ignite at all ambient&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:14.320 --> 00:18:20.880
temperatures if exposed to an ignition source.
It is a difficult fuel to handle for this reason.&nbsp;

00:18:20.880 --> 00:18:27.840
Hydrogen also has no odor and it’s flame is nearly&nbsp;
invisible, so detection of leaks is difficult.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:27.840 --> 00:18:33.840
It’s also difficult to mix odorising agents, like&nbsp;
the sulfur odorants we add to natural gas, because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:33.840 --> 00:18:38.720
the freezing temperatures of liquid hydrogen&nbsp;
would simply turn them solid in the tanks and they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:38.720 --> 00:18:45.040
wouldn’t exit with the gas when there was a leak.&nbsp;
These odorants would also contaminate any fuels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:45.040 --> 00:18:51.029
cells using hydrogen to generate electricity. [18]
This is a problem because many future hydrogen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:51.029 --> 00:18:56.880
powered jet engines, including all of&nbsp;
Airbus’ concepts, call for hybrid engines,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:56.880 --> 00:19:02.480
mixing electric motors powered by hydrogen&nbsp;
fuel cells with combustion turbines burning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:02.480 --> 00:19:07.680
hydrogen. [20] Gas alarms will be essential&nbsp;
early warning systems and they will need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:07.680 --> 00:19:13.360
to be located anywhere large quantities of&nbsp;
hydrogen are stored. In the case of a leak,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:13.360 --> 00:19:19.040
modular tanks, with shut off valves between&nbsp;
each section will be essential to minimize risk.&nbsp;

00:19:19.680 --> 00:19:25.440
These storage and handling difficulties are likely&nbsp;
the largest barrier for hydrogen moving forward,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:25.440 --> 00:19:29.920
and this is why some have proposed an&nbsp;
extra step, that will use hydrogen to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:29.920 --> 00:19:36.640
generate a new type of hydrocarbon fuel. E-Fuels.
This would be done by combining carbon dioxide,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:36.640 --> 00:19:41.200
which will be drawn directly from the&nbsp;
atmosphere using direct air capture,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:41.200 --> 00:19:47.200
with hydrogen to produce methanol. This methanol&nbsp;
would be liquid at ambient temperatures and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:47.200 --> 00:19:54.080
could be further processed, like our ethanol from&nbsp;
earlier, to produce kerosene efuels. E-Fuels are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:54.080 --> 00:20:00.960
fuels that are created entirely using sustainable&nbsp;
feedstocks and renewable electricity. This would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:00.960 --> 00:20:07.280
solve the scalability issues of biofuels, but&nbsp;
more than likely cost a lot more due to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:07.280 --> 00:20:13.760
sheer amount of energy needed to both create&nbsp;
hydrogen and draw carbon dioxide from the air.&nbsp;

00:20:13.760 --> 00:20:17.360
It’s hard to make predictions on the&nbsp;
future of the air travel industry.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:18.240 --> 00:20:24.720
If I was placing bets, I think biofuel mandates,&nbsp;
despite their questionable environmental benefit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:24.720 --> 00:20:31.040
will continue to be introduced, and then, as&nbsp;
excess renewable electricity floods the market,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:31.040 --> 00:20:36.720
energy intensive processes like efuels may&nbsp;
take over. Primarily because these fuels are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:36.720 --> 00:20:43.200
compatible with current jet engines. Hydrogen&nbsp;
has a chance of succeeding, but it will require&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:43.200 --> 00:20:48.080
massive investments to completely&nbsp;
overhaul airport and plane architecture,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:48.080 --> 00:20:53.360
which alone will cost trillions of dollars.
This cost barrier is going to be something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:53.360 --> 00:20:59.920
the aviation industry is going to have to accept&nbsp;
in the near term. It’s more than likely that air&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:59.920 --> 00:21:06.080
travel will get vastly more expensive during&nbsp;
this transitional period. That cost inflation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:06.080 --> 00:21:12.240
can be minimized by a gradual introduction of&nbsp;
biofuels and efuels that are compatible with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:12.240 --> 00:21:18.880
current generation infrastructure. However,&nbsp;
as we saw in Norway, even just a 0.5% biofuel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:18.880 --> 00:21:26.400
mandate increased fuel costs significantly. And&nbsp;
this may just be a hard truth we as a society&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:26.400 --> 00:21:32.800
need to accept if we truly want to become a&nbsp;
carbon neutral civilisation and save our planet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:32.800 --> 00:21:38.560
that the aviation industry's historic decline&nbsp;
in ticket prices may be beginning to reverse.&nbsp;

00:21:39.280 --> 00:21:44.800
There is one facet to the future of aviation&nbsp;
fuel that I have not mentioned in this video. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:44.800 --> 00:21:51.040
electric future. There are several small planes&nbsp;
already in flying, powered by batteries. Their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:51.040 --> 00:21:56.960
